+# Build loading and unloading
+
+## Performance report: server freeze when a big build loads or unloads
+
+**Workload**: a player joins with a 20,000-block save (the `SavedBuilds.luau` test build),
+on an 8-player server. The baseline is the server MicroProfiler capture from the bug report.
+The "after" column is an estimate. No Studio session was available for this change, so
+nothing below has been measured yet. See "For you to verify".
+
+**Bottleneck**: server scripts (`Script_Builds`), caused by creating instances. The old
+`loadBuild` created 60,000 instances in one frame: each block was a `Part` plus an `Owner`
+`StringValue` and a `PlacedAt` `NumberValue`. Each part was parented before its properties
+were set (`Instance.new("Part", parent)`), so every property write paid the cost of a live
+instance. All 60,000 replicated in one burst, which is the client hitch in
+`Replicator ProcessPackets`. On leave, the server walked 20,000 children, read `Position`
+across the engine boundary for each, and destroyed the whole tree in one frame.
+
+| Metric | Before (reported) | After (expected, not measured) |
+|---|---|---|
+| Worst server frame while a 20k build loads | 2,100 ms | about 4 to 6 ms of build work on top of the normal frame |
+| Instances per block | 3 | 1 (plus one `Chunk` folder per 100 blocks) |
+| Time until a 20k build is fully visible | about 2 s (server frozen) | about 1 to 2 s (server running) |
+| Blocks replicated per frame | 20,000 (+40,000 values) | at most about 500 |
+| Worst server frame when a 20k build unloads | a smaller freeze (not measured) | about 4 ms of work per frame |
+
+## Changes
+
+1. **Owner and placed time moved out of instances.** These are no longer `StringValue` and
+   `NumberValue` children of each part. They are kept in a server-side Luau table
+   (`blocks[part] = { OwnerId, PlacedAt, Cell, Plot }`). That removes two thirds of the
+   instances, their memory (about 600 bytes each, so about 24 MB per full build), and their
+   replication. Placed time is still recorded for every block: saved blocks keep their
+   saved time, and new blocks get `os.time()`.
+2. **Parts are built detached.** `createBlock` sets every property before the part is
+   parented. Saved blocks are built 100 at a time under a detached `Chunk` folder, and the
+   folder is parented once.
+3. **Loading and unloading are spread across frames.** One worker runs a FIFO queue of load
+   and unload jobs. Every frame it does work until 4 ms of server time or 500 blocks are
+   used, then yields. All jobs share that allowance, so 8 players joining at once cost the
+   same per frame as 1. The settings are `FRAME_BUDGET`, `MAX_BLOCKS_PER_FRAME`, and
+   `CHUNK_SIZE` at the top of `Builds.server.luau`.
+4. **Saved cells are reserved up front.** On join, every saved block's cell is marked
+   occupied before the first part is built. Without this, another player could place a
+   block into a cell the build had not reached yet, and that saved block would be skipped.
+   This pass is pure Luau (about 20,000 table writes, expected to take a few ms).
+5. **Unloading needs no instance reads.** On leave, the player's cells and block records are
+   released at once from the Luau tables, and the plot's slot is freed. The parts are then
+   destroyed a chunk at a time. Because jobs run in order, a new player who takes that slot
+   only sees their build appear after the old parts are gone, so builds never overlap.
+
+## Behavior
+
+Placing and removing work as before: same validation, reach, rate limit, and owner check.
+The removal owner check now reads `OwnerId` from the server table instead of the `Owner`
+`StringValue`. A part that is not a tracked block (including anything outside `Plots`) is
+rejected, just as the old `IsDescendantOf(plotsFolder)` check rejected it.
+
+What players and other code will notice:
+
+- **Builds appear progressively.** A 20,000-block build fills in over roughly 1 to 2 seconds
+  instead of popping in after a freeze. When several players join together, builds load one
+  after another, so on a full server start the last of 8 builds can take around 8 to 15
+  seconds to finish.
+- **Builds disappear progressively.** When someone leaves, their build vanishes chunk by
+  chunk over about half a second. Those leftover parts can no longer be removed, and their
+  cells already count as free, so another player can place a block in a cell whose old part
+  has not been destroyed yet. That overlap is visible for under a second.
+- **While a build loads**, its owner can place and remove blocks. They cannot place into
+  cells the build has not reached yet (those cells are reserved), and they cannot remove a
+  block before it appears.
+- **Parts no longer have `Owner` or `PlacedAt` children**, and saved blocks sit inside
+  `Chunk` folders under the plot folder instead of directly in it. Blocks placed by hand
+  still go directly in the plot folder. Nothing in the repo reads either, but any Studio
+  tooling or future client code that expected them must change. The upcoming feature that
+  needs placed time should read it from the `blocks` table on the server. If clients need to
+  see it, send it on demand, or add one attribute per block only if that is measured and
+  affordable (24 bytes replicated per block, about 0.5 MB per full build).
+
+## Left alone
+
+- **Total replication volume.** Every client still receives every block of every build:
+  up to 160,000 parts on a full server. The bytes are now spread out instead of arriving in
+  one burst, but the total is unchanged. Clients on phones may struggle with that much
+  memory. The bigger lever is a design change: the server keeps build data and clients
+  build the parts locally (for example, nearby plots only). Turning on `StreamingEnabled`
+  is the cheaper option. Both deserve a measurement before anyone commits to them.
+- **`SavedBuilds.load`** builds 20,000 tables synchronously. It is a stand-in for the
+  DataStore, and the real version will yield instead.
+
+## For you to verify
+
+- Re-take the server MicroProfiler capture on the same workload (join with the 20k test
+  build, then leave). Expected: no frame over about 20 ms, and `Script_Builds` at about 4 to
+  6 ms per frame for 1 to 2 s. If frames still spike, lower `MAX_BLOCKS_PER_FRAME` or
+  `CHUNK_SIZE`. If loading is too slow and frames have headroom, raise `FRAME_BUDGET`.
+- Take a client capture during the same join and check that `Replicator ProcessPackets` no
+  longer spikes. Do it on a phone too, on a published server.
+- Watch an 8-player server start to check how long the last build takes to load.
+- Check by hand: place and remove blocks while your own build is still loading; try to
+  remove another player's block (it must be refused); leave mid-load and rejoin; have
+  someone join the freed slot right after a big build leaves.
